Title: account posture and podman-unshare compose in the wrong order: sudo fails inside the rootless namespace
Created: 2026-09-04
Author: MinhHaDuong

--- log ---
2026-09-04T05:34Z MinhHaDuong created

--- body ---
## Context

Ticket 0625 ratified (DECISIONS.md, 2026-09-03) that no target process may run
as the operator: `bench/acceptance/posture.py`'s `Posture.wrap()` prepends
`sudo -n -u tester --preserve-env=<names> -- <argv>` at exactly one seam, each
adapter's own target-process spawn (`zoteus.py::running()` and its siblings).
Separately, `bench/acceptance/sandbox.py`'s `PodmanUnshare` mechanism gives
`R10-no-egress` its network isolation by wrapping a re-invocation of the whole
driver in `--drive` mode: `podman unshare unshare -n -- python3 run.py
--adapter zoteus --arena <a> --drive` (`assertions.py::check_no_egress`'s
`subject = run_traced(drive_argv, mechanism=mechanism, network_shared=False,
...)`, `sandbox.py::run_traced`, `PodmanUnshare.isolated()`).

These two wraps compose in the wrong order for this one check. The account
switch happens INSIDE the already-entered podman rootless user namespace
(the adapter's `_posture.wrap()` call fires from within the re-invoked
`--drive` process), and `sudo` cannot run there — reproduced directly on this
machine (padme, 2026-09-04):

    $ podman unshare unshare -n -- sudo -n -u tester --preserve-env=X -- true
    sudo: /etc/sudo.conf is owned by uid 65534, should be 0
    sudo: ouverture de /etc/sudoers impossible: Permission non accordée

Inside `podman unshare`, the invoking user is remapped to fake-root within the
namespace (`podman unshare whoami` → `root`), but the REAL host files
`sudo` checks (`/etc/sudo.conf`, `/etc/sudoers`, both really owned by host
uid 0) appear owned by uid 65534 (`nogroup`) from inside the namespace's uid
mapping. `sudo` refuses on principle rather than trusting that. This is not a
missing sudoers line, ACL, or PATH issue — those were all real, separate
blockers on this same run and are already fixed (see 0629's log): the
sudoers `SETENV` rule, a `node` binary unreachable under `tester` (user-local
install, fixed with a system-wide copy outside any home directory), and the
acceptance arena needing to be both operator-creatable and tester-writable
(fixed with a default ACL: `setfacl -d -m u:tester:rwx` on
`~/data/acceptance-arena`, which is now the working `ACCEPTANCE_ARENA`).
With every one of those fixed, this is the one remaining, structural gap.

**The reordering fixes it — verified directly, this machine:**

    $ sudo -n -u tester --preserve-env=X -- podman unshare unshare -n -- true
    (warnings about no systemd user session, falls back to cgroupfs; harmless)
    $ echo $?
    0

`tester` already has subuid/subgid ranges (`/etc/subuid`, `/etc/subgid`:
`tester:165536:65536`), confirmed by `sudo -u tester podman unshare whoami`
→ `root` succeeding cleanly. So switching identity FIRST, then entering the
network namespace AS `tester`, works end to end on this machine's setup.

## The design question this raises

Ticket 0625 deliberately chose "each adapter spawns its target under the
account while the harness itself keeps running as the operator" over "the
harness re-executes itself under the account" — `posture.py`'s own module
docstring gives the reason: a full re-exec would force the harness's OWN work
(arena directories, the committed JSON artifact) across the account boundary
too, which has no business belonging to `tester`.

The `--drive` re-invocation this ticket is about is NOT that full re-exec: it
is already a narrower re-invocation that exists only to run under the
tracer+mechanism for `R10-no-egress`'s subject arm. The top-level process
(the one writing the arena directories and the committed artifact) stays
running as the operator throughout, outside this `--drive` subprocess
entirely (`assertions.py::check_no_egress` calls `run_traced` and only reads
back the RESULT). So making the WHOLE `--drive` subprocess run as `tester` —
by composing the wrap as `mechanism.isolated(posture.wrap(drive_argv, env),
...)` instead of the current `mechanism.isolated(drive_argv, ...)` with the
posture wrap applied later, deep inside, only to the final `node` spawn — does
not appear to reverse 0625's actual ruling. Everything the `--drive`
subprocess itself does (seed the data dir, spawn the target, drive verbs) is
target-owned derived state that `tester` is supposed to own anyway ("write
access to nothing but the arena").

This reasoning is offered as a starting hypothesis, not a decision — it needs
verification against what `--drive` mode actually does end to end (does
anything in that path read/write something only the operator should touch?),
and the same question applies to `Bubblewrap`, the other `Mechanism` in
`sandbox.MECHANISMS` (currently unusable in this executor per prior notes —
"bwrap cannot start inside the executor" — but should be checked for the same
ordering trap so a future machine where bubblewrap DOES work doesn't hit
this again).

## Relevant files

- `bench/acceptance/posture.py` — `Posture.wrap()` (~line 142), `_works()`
  (~line 233), `resolve()` (~line 258): the account-switch seam and its
  probe, resolved fresh per process.
- `bench/acceptance/sandbox.py` — `PodmanUnshare.isolated()`/`.shared()`
  (~line 235), `Bubblewrap` (the other mechanism, ~line 152/180),
  `run_traced()` (~line 379): where a mechanism wraps an argv.
- `bench/acceptance/assertions.py` — `check_no_egress()` (~line 180): the one
  caller that re-invokes the driver itself as `drive_argv` under a mechanism,
  rather than wrapping a target's own spawn directly.
- `bench/acceptance/run.py` — `drive_argv_for()` (~line 459): builds the
  `--drive` re-invocation argv that gets mechanism-wrapped.
- `bench/acceptance/adapters/zoteus.py` — `running()` (~line 358): where
  `self._posture.wrap(cmd, ...)` currently fires, from inside the already-
  entered namespace when reached via `--drive` under `PodmanUnshare`.
- `bench/results/0629-gap-a/` — the ticket blocked on this (0629's Action 2,
  `ZOTEUS_UPDATE_CHECK=false` expecting `dns: 0`); its own attribution does
  NOT depend on this fix (three other measurements already confirm it
  independently), so this is not urgent for 0629's own sake, but it also
  blocks every other check needing a target spawn under isolation (`R13`,
  `R22`, `R3`, `R23` all showed `NOT-RUN` in the same run, same root cause).

## Actions

1. Confirm (read `--drive` mode's actual code path, `run.py` + the adapter's
   `install`/`configure`/`query` verbs as driven for `zoteus`) that nothing in
   it needs operator identity — i.e. the design-question hypothesis above
   actually holds. If something does, the fix needs a narrower seam instead
   (e.g., a mechanism-aware `Posture` that knows to switch identity before a
   specific mechanism enters its namespace, rather than reordering the whole
   `--drive` subprocess).
2. Implement the reordering (or the narrower alternative if Action 1 finds a
   reason to prefer it) so `R10-no-egress`'s subject arm composes as
   identity-switch-then-namespace, not the reverse.
3. Check `Bubblewrap` for the same trap, even though it's not usable on this
   machine today — a future machine where it works should not silently hit
   this again.
4. Re-run ticket 0629's confirming arm
   (`bench/results/0629-gap-a/README.md` § 3's recipe, arena now at
   `~/data/acceptance-arena`) and tick its two blocked verification boxes
   once `R10-no-egress` actually runs (not `not-run`) against this machine.

## Test

Red-first: a test that drives `PodmanUnshare.isolated()` (or the composed
wrap) against a trivial command needing BOTH the namespace and a real account
switch, asserting it succeeds — this should fail against the current
(wrong-order) composition and pass after the fix. If the fix is source-level
reorder rather than something unit-testable without a real `tester` account
and podman on the test machine, mark it `@pytest.mark.integration` and skip
cleanly (not silently pass) where the account/mechanism preconditions aren't
met, mirroring how `posture.resolve()` itself reports refusal rather than
faking success.

## Verification

- [ ] `R10-no-egress` on `zoteus` with `ZOTEUS_UPDATE_CHECK=false` returns
      PASS or FAIL, not NOT-RUN, on this machine.
- [ ] `R13`, `R22`, `R3`, `R23` (the other checks needing a target spawn under
      isolation) also stop reporting NOT-RUN for this same reason.
- [ ] `make check` passes.
- [ ] Ticket 0629's two blocked verification checkboxes are ticked, or the
      ticket records why they still cannot be, with a different reason than
      today's.

## Invariants

- Ticket 0625's actual ruling holds: no target process runs as the operator,
  ever, on any code path — this fix must not weaken that, only fix the
  ordering of two isolations that both already exist.
- `sudo`'s own security check (refusing when it can't verify real uid-0
  ownership of its config) stays untouched — the fix works WITH it, not
  around it (no `--preserve-env`-style workaround that would reopen the
  channel ticket 0625's Action 1 closed).
- The operator-owned arena directories and the committed JSON artifact keep
  being written as the operator, exactly as today.

## Exit criteria

`R10-no-egress`'s subject arm (and the other checks sharing this composition)
actually run — pass or fail on their own merits — under the `podman-unshare`
mechanism combined with the `account` posture on this machine, with the
account-posture invariant from ticket 0625 intact and a test that would have
caught the wrong-order regression.
